t from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); i.e. it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Restrictions on a sunny line\n\nIf a line contains two points of \\(T_{3}\\) having the same\n\\(x\\)-coordinate, the line is vertical (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis) and\nhence non‑sunny.  \nSimilarly, two points with the same \\(y\\)-coordinate force the line to be\nhorizontal (non‑sunny), and two points with the same value of \\(x+y\\) force\nthe line to have slope \\(-1\\) (non‑sunny).  \n\nConsequently a sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(T_{3}\\), and\nany admissible pair must have **pairwise distinct** \\(x\\)-coordinates,\n\\(y\\)-coordinates and sums \\(x+y\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  All admissible pairs\n\nChecking all \\(\\binom{6}{2}=15\\) pairs, the only ones satisfying the\ndistinctness condition are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{1}&=\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\},\\\\\nP_{2}&=\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\},\\\\\nP_{3}&=\\{(1,3),(2,1)\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nNo other pair of points from \\(T_{3}\\) can lie on a sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Using two sunny lines\n\nWe have to employ exactly two sunny lines.  \nEach sunny line can cover at most one of the three admissible pairs, so the\ntwo sunny lines together can cover at most four points of \\(T_{3}\\).\n\nChoose the pairs \\(P_{1}\\) and \\(P_{2}\\) for the sunny lines.  \nThe uncovered points are then \\((1,3)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  Both have the same\nsum \\(x+y=4\\); therefore they lie on the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{3}:\\;x+y=4,\n\\]\n\nwhich is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and hence non‑sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Explicit equations\n\n* **Sunny line \\(L_{1}\\)** through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\):\n  \\[\n  y=x\\qquad(\\text{slope }1\\neq0,\\infty,-1).\n  \\]\n\n* **Sunny line \\(L_{2}\\)** through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\):\n  \\[\n  y-2=-\\frac12(x-1)\\;\\Longrightarrow\\; y=-\\frac12x+\\frac52\n  \\qquad(\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12\\neq0,\\infty,-1).\n  \\]\n\n* **Non‑sunny line \\(L_{3}\\)** through \\((1,3)\\) and \\((2,1)\\):\n  \\[\n  x+y=4\\qquad(\\text{slope }-1).\n  \\]\n\nAll three lines are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Verification of coverage\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{Point} & \\text{Line containing it} \\\\ \\hline\n(1,1) & L_{1} \\\\\n(2,2) & L_{1} \\\\\n(1,2) & L_{2} \\\\\n(3,1) & L_{2} \\\\\n(1,3) & L_{3} \\\\\n(2,1) & L_{3}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{3}\\) lies on at least one of the three lines,\nexactly two of the lines (\\(L_{1},L_{2}\\)) are sunny, and the third line\n(\\(L_{3}\\)) is non‑sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) there exists a set of three distinct lines with exactly\n\\(k=2\\) sunny lines that satisfies the required covering condition.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{A configuration exists for }n=3,\\;k=2.}\n\\]"
