            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{3}=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le 4\\}\n      =\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\); equivalently its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Restrictions on a sunny line\n\nConsider two distinct points \\((a,b),(c,d)\\in T_{3}\\).\n\n* If \\(a=c\\) the line is vertical (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis) → non‑sunny.  \n* If \\(b=d\\) the line is horizontal (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis) → non‑sunny.  \n* If \\(a+b=c+d\\) then the slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{d-b}{c-a}= -1\\) (since \\(d-b=-(c-a)\\)), so the line is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) → non‑sunny.\n\nHence a sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(T_{3}\\), and any pair of points that can lie on a sunny line must satisfy  \n\n\\[\na\\neq c,\\qquad b\\neq d,\\qquad a+b\\neq c+d. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  All admissible pairs\n\nWe examine all \\(\\binom{6}{2}=15\\) unordered pairs of points in \\(T_{3}\\) and keep those satisfying (1).\n\n| Pair | \\(x\\)‑coordinates | \\(y\\)‑coordinates | Sums | Satisfies (1)? |\n|------|------------------|------------------|------|----------------|\n| (1,1),(1,2) | equal | – | – | No (vertical) |\n| (1,1),(1,3) | equal | – | – | No |\n| (1,1),(2,1) | – | equal | – | No (horizontal) |\n| (1,1),(3,1) | – | equal | – | No |\n| (1,1),(2,2) | 1≠2, 1≠2, 2≠4 | ✓ | ✓ |\n| (1,1),(3,2) – not in \\(T_{3}\\) |\n| (1,2),(1,3) | equal | – | – | No |\n| (1,2),(2,1) | 1≠2, 2≠1, 3≠3 | **fails** sum equality |\n| (1,2),(2,2) | – | equal | – | No |\n| (1,2),(3,1) | 1≠3, 2≠1, 3≠4 | ✓ | ✓ |\n| (1,3),(2,1) | 1≠2, 3≠1, 4=3? actually 1+3=4,2+1=3 → sums differ | ✓ | ✓ |\n| (1,3),(2,2) | 1≠2, 3≠2, 4≠4? sums equal → fails |\n| (1,3),(3,1) | 1≠3, 3≠1, 4=4 → fails |\n| (2,1),(2,2) | equal | – | – | No |\n| (2,1),(3,1) | – | equal | – | No |\n| (2,2),(3,1) | 2≠3, 2≠1, 4≠4 → fails |\n\nThe only pairs satisfying (1) are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{1}&=\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\},\\\\\nP_{2}&=\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\},\\\\\nP_{3}&=\\{(1,3),(2,1)\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Using exactly two sunny lines\n\nWe must have exactly two sunny lines, each of which can cover at most one of the admissible pairs.  \nChoosing any two of the three pairs covers four points of \\(T_{3}\\); the two remaining points have the same sum \\(x+y\\) and therefore lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\), which is non‑sunny.\n\nSelect \\(P_{1}\\) and \\(P_{2}\\) for the sunny lines.  \nThe uncovered points are \\((1,3)\\) and \\((2,1)\\); both satisfy \\(x+y=4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Explicit equations of the three lines\n\n* **Sunny line \\(L_{1}\\)** through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\):\n  \\[\n  y=x \\qquad(\\text{slope }1\\neq0,\\infty,-1).\n  \\]\n\n* **Sunny line \\(L_{2}\\)** through \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\):\n  \\[\n  y-2=-\\frac12(x-1)\\;\\Longrightarrow\\; y=-\\frac12x+\\frac52\n  \\qquad(\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12\\neq0,\\infty,-1).\n  \\]\n\n* **Non‑sunny line \\(L_{3}\\)** through \\((1,3)\\) and \\((2,1)\\):\n  \\[\n  x+y=4 \\qquad(\\text{slope }-1,\\text{ parallel to }x+y=0).\n  \\]\n\nAll three lines are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Verification of coverage\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{Point} & \\text{Line containing it} \\\\ \\hline\n(1,1) & L_{1} \\\\\n(2,2) & L_{1} \\\\\n(1,2) & L_{2} \\\\\n(3,1) & L_{2} \\\\\n(1,3) & L_{3} \\\\\n(2,1) & L_{3}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThus every point of \\(T_{3}\\) lies on at least one of the three lines, exactly two of the lines (\\(L_{1},L_{2}\\)) are sunny, and the third line (\\(L_{3}\\)) is non‑sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion\n\nA set of three distinct lines with exactly two sunny lines satisfying the required covering condition exists for \\(n=3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3,\\;k=2\\text{ is attainable.}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
