nWithin the set \\(\\{1,2,3\\}\\) for each coordinate, a vertical line can meet at most the three points with that \\(x\\)-value, a horizontal line at most the three points with that \\(y\\)-value, and a line of slope \\(-1\\) at most the three points whose sums equal the constant \\(c\\). ∎\n\nWe distinguish two cases according to the number of points of \\(T_3\\) lying on \\(\\ell_s\\).\n\n---\n\n**Case A.** \\(\\ell_s\\) contains **three** points of \\(T_3\\).\n\nThe remaining three points are the complement of a vertical, horizontal, or diagonal triple.  \nA direct inspection shows that in each of the three possibilities the three leftover points are pairwise non‑sunny:\n\n* If \\(\\ell_s\\) is vertical \\(x=1\\), the leftovers are \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\); any two of them share a common \\(x\\), a common \\(y\\), or lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\).  \n* If \\(\\ell_s\\) is horizontal \\(y=1\\), the leftovers are \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\); again each pair is non‑sunny.  \n* If \\(\\ell_s\\) is diagonal \\(x+y=4\\), the leftovers are \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\); each pair is non‑sunny.\n\nHence each sunny line can cover **at most one** of the three remaining points, so two sunny lines cannot cover all three. Contradiction.\n\n---\n\n**Case B.** \\(\\ell_s\\) contains **exactly two** points of \\(T_3\\).\n\nAll possible non‑sunny pairs are:\n\n* vertical: \\((1,1)-(1,2),\\;(1,2)-(1,3),\\;(2,1)-(2,2)\\);\n* horizontal: \\((1,1)-(2,1),\\;(2,1)-(3,1),\\;(1,2)-(2,2)\\);\n* diagonal (slope \\(-1\\)): \\((1,2)-(2,1),\\;(1,3)-(2,2),\\;(2,2)-(3,1)\\).\n\nFor each such pair we list the four points that remain and verify that among those four there is **always** a non‑sunny pair, i.e. two points whose connecting line has slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\). Consequently the four points cannot be split into two sunny pairs; at least three sunny lines would be required.\n\nA complete table is given below.\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c}\n\\text{Non‑sunny pair }(\\ell_s) & \\text{Remaining points} & \\text{Non‑sunny pair among them}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1)-(1,2) & (1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1) & (2,1)-(3,1)\\text{ horizontal}\\\\\n(1,2)-(1,3) & (1,1),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1) & (2,1)-(2,2)\\text{ vertical}\\\\\n(2,1)-(2,2) & (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1) & (1,2)-(3,1)\\text{ slope }-1/2\\ (\\text{sunny})\\text{ but }(1,1)-(1,3)\\text{ vertical}\\\\\n(1,1)-(2,1) & (1,2),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1) & (1,2)-(2,2)\\text{ horizontal}\\\\\n(2,1)-(3,1) & (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,2) & (1,1)-(1,3)\\text{ vertical}\\\\\n(1,2)-(2,2) & (1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1) & (2,1)-(3,1)\\text{ horizontal}\\\\\n(1,2)-(2,1) & (1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1) & (1,1)-(2,2)\\text{ slope }1\\ (\\text{sunny})\\text{ but }(1,3)-(3,1)\\text{ slope }-1\\\\\n(1,3)-(2,2) & (1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(3,1) & (1,2)-(2,1)\\text{ slope }-1\\\\\n(2,2)-(3,1) & (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1) & (1,2)-(2,1)\\text{ slope }-1\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nIn every row at least one of the four remaining points is forced to share a non‑sunny line with another, preventing a partition into two sunny pairs. Hence two sunny lines cannot cover the four points, contradicting the assumption.\n\n---\n\nBoth cases lead to contradictions; therefore a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince the only values of \\(k\\) that admit a construction are \\(0,1,3\\), we have completely determined the answer for the minimal case \\(n=3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,\\;1,\\;3\\}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
