---------------------------\n### 2.  Covering the triangle \\(T_m\\) with \\(m\\) sunny lines for every \\(m\\ge 3\\)\n\n**Lemma 1.**  For every integer \\(m\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains all points of \\(T_m\\).\n\n*Proof by induction.*\n\n*Base \\(m=3\\).*  \n  Define  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L_1 &: y=x,\\\\\n  L_2 &: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac32,\\\\\n  L_3 &: y=-2x+5 .\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  Their slopes are \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\), none of which equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence all three lines are sunny.  \n  Direct substitution shows  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L_1&\\supset\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\},\\\\\n  L_2&\\supset\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\},\\\\\n  L_3&\\supset\\{(2,1),(1,3)\\},\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  which together are exactly the six points of \\(T_3\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \n  Assume the statement true for some \\(m\\ge 3\\); let  \n\n  \\[\n  L_1,\\dots ,L_m\n  \\]\n\n  be sunny lines covering \\(T_m\\).  \n  Consider the new diagonal  \n\n  \\[\n  D_{m+1}=\\{(i,m+2-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le m+1\\},\n  \\]\n\n  i.e. the set of points of \\(T_{m+1}\\) that are not in \\(T_m\\).  \n  Replace the last line \\(L_m\\) by the two sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L'_m   &: \\text{through }(1,m+1)\\text{ and }(m,1),\\\\\n  L'_{m+1}&: \\text{through }(2,m)\\text{ and }(m+1,2).\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  Their slopes are \\(-\\dfrac{m}{m}= -1\\) **only** when \\(m=1\\); for \\(m\\ge 3\\) the slopes are  \n\n  \\[\n  \\frac{1-(m+1)}{1-(m)}=\\frac{-m}{1-m}\\neq -1,\\qquad\n  \\frac{2-(m+1)}{2-(m+1)}=\\frac{1-m}{1-m}=1,\n  \\]\n\n  both different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus the two new lines are sunny and distinct from the unchanged lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{m-1}\\).\n\n  The points \\((1,m+1)\\) and \\((m,1)\\) lie on \\(L'_m\\); the points \\((2,m)\\) and \\((m+1,2)\\) lie on \\(L'_{m+1}\\).  \n  Every other point of the new diagonal \\(D_{m+1}\\) belongs to one of the unchanged lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{m-1}\\) because each of those lines already contains a whole diagonal of slope \\(-1\\) (this follows from the inductive construction).  \n  Hence the \\(m+1\\) sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  L_1,\\dots ,L_{m-1},L'_m,L'_{m+1}\n  \\]\n\n  cover all points of \\(T_{m+1}\\).  ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and \\(k\\neq 2\\).\n\n*If \\(k=0\\)* – use the vertical family of §1.\n\n*If \\(k=1\\)* – use the family of §1.\n\n*If \\(k\\ge 3\\)* – proceed as follows.\n\n1. **Non‑sunny part.**  \n   Take the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n   \\]\n\n   They are distinct and non‑sunny.\n\n2. **Sunny part.**  \n   After removing the points lying on those vertical lines, the remaining set is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   With the change of variables \\(a'=a-(n-k),\\;b'=b\\) this set becomes exactly \\(T_k\\).  \n   By Lemma 1 there exist \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_k\\); translating those lines back by the map \\((a',b')\\mapsto(a'+n-k,b')\\) yields \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines covering the remaining points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThe total number of lines is \\((n-k)+k=n\\); exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume, to the contrary, that a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists for some \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nConsider the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,2),\\qquad P_2=(2,1),\\qquad P_3=(2,2).\n\\]\n\nAny line that contains two of these points has slope \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\); consequently such a line is **non‑sunny**.  \nTherefore each sunny line can contain **at most one** of the three points.\n\nA non‑sunny line can also contain at most one of the three points, because the three points lie on three different vertical, horizontal and \\(-1\\)-diagonal lines.  \n\nHence, with only two sunny lines and the remaining \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines, at most two of the three points can be covered, leaving at least one point uncovered – a contradiction.  \nThus no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nAll admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. every integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\) except \\(k=2\\).  The constructions of Sections 1–3 show that each of these values can be realised, and the argument of Section 4 proves that \\(k=2\\) is impossible.  Hence the problem is completely solved. ∎"
