            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nThus the three families of non‑sunny lines are\n\n* vertical lines \\(x=c\\);\n* horizontal lines \\(y=c\\);\n* lines of slope \\(-1\\), i.e. \\(x+y=c\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n#### \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_i\\) is non‑sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_a\\).  \nHence the family \\(\\{\\ell_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and contains no sunny line.\n\n#### \\(k=1\\)\n\nTake the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n-1).\n\\]\n\nThese are non‑sunny.  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\le n-1\\) lies on \\(\\ell_a\\).  \nIf \\(a=n\\) then the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b=1\\); thus the only point not covered by the vertical lines is \\((n,1)\\).\n\nChoose any sunny line passing through \\((n,1)\\); for definiteness let  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;y = 2(x-n)+1 = 2x-2n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell\\) is \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.  \nNow every point of \\(T_n\\) lies either on one of the vertical lines or on \\(\\ell\\).  \nExactly one of the \\(n\\) lines is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly two sunny lines.\n\nConsider the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,2),\\qquad P_2=(2,1),\\qquad P_3=(2,2),\n\\]\nall of which belong to \\(T_n\\) (since \\(1+2\\le n+1\\) and \\(2+2\\le n+1\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\)).\n\n*If a line contains two of the points \\(P_1,P_2,P_3\\), its slope is*\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{slope}(P_1P_2)&=0\\quad(\\text{horizontal}),\\\\\n\\text{slope}(P_1P_3)&=-1,\\\\\n\\text{slope}(P_2P_3)&=\\infty\\quad(\\text{vertical}),\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n*all of which are forbidden for a sunny line.*  \nHence **each sunny line can contain at most one** of the three points.\n\nNow observe that the three points lie on three distinct non‑sunny lines:\n\\[\nP_1\\in\\{y=2\\},\\qquad P_2\\in\\{x=2\\},\\qquad P_3\\in\\{x+y=4\\}.\n\\]\nConsequently a non‑sunny line can also contain at most one of the three points.\n\nThus, using the two sunny lines together with the remaining \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines, we can cover at most two of the three points \\(P_1,P_2,P_3\\).  \nAt least one of them would remain uncovered, contradicting the hypothesis that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on some line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nTherefore a configuration with exactly two sunny lines cannot exist for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\n*   The values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are realizable for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) (explicit constructions given in §1).  \n*   The value \\(k=2\\) is impossible for every integer \\(n