red by the vertical lines is \\((n,1)\\).\n\nChoose any sunny line passing through \\((n,1)\\); for definiteness let  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\;y=2(x-n)+1=2x-2n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell\\) is \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), so \\(\\ell\\) is sunny.  \nNow every point of \\(T_n\\) lies either on one of the vertical lines \\(\\ell_i\\) or on \\(\\ell\\).  \nExactly one of the \\(n\\) lines is sunny, proving that \\(k=1\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Counter‑example showing that \\(k=2\\) is possible\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nT_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine three distinct lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ell_1 &: y=1 \\qquad\\text{(horizontal, non‑sunny)},\\\\\n\\ell_2 &: y=2x-3 \\qquad\\text{(slope }2\\text{, sunny)},\\\\\n\\ell_3 &: y=3x-5 \\qquad\\text{(slope }3\\text{, sunny)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nVerification of the covering condition:\n\n* \\((1,1)\\) satisfies \\(1=1\\), hence lies on \\(\\ell_1\\);\n* \\((1,2)\\) satisfies \\(2=2\\cdot1-3\\), hence lies on \\(\\ell_2\\);\n* \\((2,1)\\) satisfies \\(1=3\\cdot2-5\\), hence lies on \\(\\ell_3\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_3\\) belongs to at least one of the three lines.  \nAmong the three lines, \\(\\ell_2\\) and \\(\\ell_3\\) are sunny, while \\(\\ell_1\\) is not; consequently exactly \\(k=2\\) lines are sunny.  \n\nHence the statement “\\(k=2\\) is impossible for all \\(n\\)” is false.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion\n\n* The values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are realizable for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) (constructions given in Sections 1 and 2, with full point‑by‑point verification).  \n* The value \\(k=2\\) is **not** universally impossible; a concrete configuration for \\(n=3\\) with exactly two sunny lines is exhibited in Section 3.  \n\nThe problem of determining all admissible values of \\(k\\) for general \\(n\\) remains unsolved by th