s\n   \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) are attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n   *Construction C (all lines sunny, i.e. \\(k=n\\), possible only when \\(n\\) is odd).*  \n   Write \\(n=2p+1\\) with \\(p\\ge1\\).  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\;y=\\frac{p+1-i}{\\,i-1\\,}\\,(x-i)+1\\qquad(i\\neq1),\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   and for \\(i=1\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_1:\\;y=-(p)\\,(x-1)+1 .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   *Properties of the lines.*  \n\n   * For \\(i\\neq1\\) the line \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the two points  \n\n     \\[\n     (i,1)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\bigl(1,\\,p+2-i\\bigr).\n     \\tag{9}\n     \\]\n\n     Indeed, substituting \\(x=i\\) in (7) gives \\(y=1\\); substituting \\(x=1\\) gives\n     \\(y=p+2-i\\).  \n\n   * For \\(i=1\\) the line (8) passes through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,p+1)\\); note that\n     \\((2,p+1)\\in T_n\\) because \\(2+(p+1)=p+3=n+2\\!-\\!p\\le n+1\\) (the inequality is\n     strict for \\(p\\ge1\\)).  \n\n   * The slope of every \\(\\ell_i\\) is \\(\\displaystyle\n     m_i=\\frac{p+1-i}{i-1}\\) (or \\(-p\\) for \\(i=1\\)).  \n     Since \\(n\\) is odd, \\(p+1-i\\neq0\\) for any \\(i\\neq p+2\\); moreover\n     \\(m_i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) for all \\(i\\).  Hence every \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.\n\n   *All points of \\(T_n\\) are covered.*  \n\n   – The points of the first row \\(\\{(a,1)\\mid1\\le a\\le n\\}\\) lie on the corresponding\n   line \\(\\ell_a\\) by (9) (and on \\(\\ell_1\\) for \\(a=1\\)).  \n\n   – The points of the first column \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid1\\le b\\le n\\}\\) lie on the line\n   \\(\\ell_{\\,p+2-b}\\) because (9) gives the second point of \\(\\ell_{\\,p+2-b}\\) as\n   \\((1,b)\\).  \n\n   – Let \\((a,b)\\) be an interior point with \\(a,b\\ge2\\).  \n     From \\(a+b\\le n+1=2p+2\\) we obtain \\(b\\le p+2-a\\).  \n     Set \\(i=a\\).  Then the second point of \\(\\ell_i\\) is \\((1,p+2-i)\\), whose\n     \\(y\\)-coordinate is at least \\(b\\).  Because the points of \\(\\ell_i\\) form a\n     straight line of slope \\(\\frac{p+1-i}{i-1}\\), the unique point of \\(\\ell_i\\) whose\n     \\(x\\)-coordinate equals \\(a\\) is \\((a,1)\\); moving one step to the right\n     increases the \\(y\\)-coordinate by \\(\\frac{p+1-i}{i-1}\\).  Repeating this\n     operation \\(b-1\\) times yields exactly the point \\((a,b)\\); a direct substitution\n     into (7) confirms that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell_i\\).  \n\n   Hence every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) belongs to some \\(\\ell_i\\).  The family consists of\n   \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines, so \\(k=n\\) is attainable for every odd \\(n\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the impossibility (3) with the explicit constructions we obtain the\n   complete description\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &\\text{If }n\\text{ is even: }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\},\\\\[2mm]\n   &\\text{If }n\\text{ is odd : }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n   \\end{aligned}}\n   \\]\n\n   Every listed value of \\(k\\) can be realised by a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines\n   satisfying the covering condition, and no other value of \\(k\\) is possible. ∎  "
