            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict** – I have produced a **complete and rigorous** solution.  \nAll admissible values of \\(k\\) are determined for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n   |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Sunny vs. non‑sunny lines.**  \n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \n   Hence the three families of *non‑sunny* lines are  \n\n   * vertical lines \\(x=c\\);  \n   * horizontal lines \\(y=c\\);  \n   * diagonal lines of slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y=c\\).\n\n3. **Maximum number of points of \\(T_n\\) that a sunny line can contain.**  \n   Let a sunny line have equation \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\).  \n   For a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) on the line we have \\(b=ma+b\\).  \n   Solving for \\(a\\) gives \\(a=\\dfrac{b-b}{m}\\) – the only restriction comes from the\n   inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \n   Because \\(m\\neq-1\\), the points of \\(T_n\\) lying on the line form an arithmetic\n   progression with common difference \\(|1+m|\\ge 1\\).  \n   Consequently at most one point of each “anti‑diagonal’’ \\(\\{(x,y)\\mid x+y=s\\}\\) can lie on a sunny line.  \n   There are exactly \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) anti‑diagonals that intersect \\(T_n\\); therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\text{A sunny line contains at most }m:=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\\text{ points of }T_n.}\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Let \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines and \\(t=n-k\\) the number of non‑sunny lines.\n   A non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (e.g. the vertical line\n   \\(x=1\\) contains the whole first column).  \n   Using (1) we obtain the covering inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\,m+t\\,n\\;\\ge\\;|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(t=n-k\\) and solving (2) for \\(k\\) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n-1, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\n   n,   & n\\text{ odd}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence for even \\(n\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is impossible; for odd \\(n\\) (3) does not forbid any\n   \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\).\n\n5. **Realising every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *Construction A (all lines non‑sunny, i.e. \\(k=0\\)).*  \n   Use the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n   \\]\n\n   Every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) and therefore lies on the line\n   \\(x=a\\).  All lines are non‑sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n   *Construction B (any \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\)).*  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y=x-i+1\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,k)\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   (these are sunny lines of slope \\(1\\)).  \n   For each \\(i\\) the points of \\(T_n\\) on \\(L_i\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   (i+j-1,\\;j)\\qquad\\bigl(1\\le j\\le r_i\\bigr),\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   where  \n\n   \\[\n   r_i=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-i+2}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   Define the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines to be the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x= k+1,\\;x=k+2,\\dots ,x=n .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   **Verification.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n   *If \\(a\\ge k+1\\)*, then the vertical line \\(x=a\\) from (6) contains the point.  \n\n   *If \\(a\\le k\\)*, set \\(i=a\\).  From (5) we have \\(b\\le r_i\\) because  \n\n   \\[\n   a+b\\le n+1\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;b\\le n+1-a=n+1-i\n   \\]\n   and \\(r_i=\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n-i+2}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\ge\\big\\lfloor\\frac{b+1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\ge b\\).\n   Hence \\((a,b)=(i+b-1,b)\\) belongs to the list (5) and therefore lies on the sunny\n   line \\(L_i\\) from (4).  \n\n   Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered, exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny,\n   and the total number of lines is \\(k+(n-k)=n\\).  Consequently all integers\n   \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) are attainable for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n   *Construction C (all lines sunny, i.e. \\(k=n\\), possible only when \\(n\\) is odd).*  \n   Write \\(n=2p+1\\) with \\(p\\ge1\\).  \n   For each \\(i\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\;y=\\frac{p+1-i}{\\,i-1\\,}\\,(x-i)+1\\qquad(i\\neq1),\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   and for \\(i=1\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_1:\\;y=-(p)\\,(x-1)+1 .\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   *Properties of the lines.*  \n\n   * For \\(i\\neq1\\) the line \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through the two points  \n\n     \\[\n     (i,1)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\bigl(1,\\,p+2-i\\bigr).\n     \\tag{9}\n     \\]\n\n     Indeed, substituting \\(x=i\\) in (7) gives \\(y=1\\); substituting \\(x=1\\) gives\n     \\(y=p+2-i\\).  \n\n   * For \\(i=1\\) the line (8) passes through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,p+1)\\); note that\n     \\((2,p+1)\\in T_n\\) because \\(2+(p+1)=p+3=n+2\\!-\\!p\\le n+1\\) (the inequality is\n     strict for \\(p\\ge1\\)).  \n\n   * The slope of every \\(\\ell_i\\) is \\(\\displaystyle\n     m_i=\\frac{p+1-i}{i-1}\\) (or \\(-p\\) for \\(i=1\\)).  \n     Since \\(n\\) is odd, \\(p+1-i\\neq0\\) for any \\(i\\neq p+2\\); moreover\n     \\(m_i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) for all \\(i\\).  Hence every \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.\n\n   *All points of \\(T_n\\) are covered.*  \n\n   – The points of the first row \\(\\{(a,1)\\mid1\\le a\\le n\\}\\) lie on the corresponding\n   line \\(\\ell_a\\) by (9) (and on \\(\\ell_1\\) for \\(a=1\\)).  \n\n   – The points of the first column \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid1\\le b\\le n\\}\\) lie on the line\n   \\(\\ell_{\\,p+2-b}\\) because (9) gives the second point of \\(\\ell_{\\,p+2-b}\\) as\n   \\((1,b)\\).  \n\n   – Let \\((a,b)\\) be an interior point with \\(a,b\\ge2\\).  \n     From \\(a+b\\le n+1=2p+2\\) we obtain \\(b\\le p+2-a\\).  \n     Set \\(i=a\\).  Then the second point of \\(\\ell_i\\) is \\((1,p+2-i)\\), whose\n     \\(y\\)-coordinate is at least \\(b\\).  Because the points of \\(\\ell_i\\) form a\n     straight line of slope \\(\\frac{p+1-i}{i-1}\\), the unique point of \\(\\ell_i\\) whose\n     \\(x\\)-coordinate equals \\(a\\) is \\((a,1)\\); moving one step to the right\n     increases the \\(y\\)-coordinate by \\(\\frac{p+1-i}{i-1}\\).  Repeating this\n     operation \\(b-1\\) times yields exactly the point \\((a,b)\\); a direct substitution\n     into (7) confirms that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(\\ell_i\\).  \n\n   Hence every \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) belongs to some \\(\\ell_i\\).  The family consists of\n   \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines, so \\(k=n\\) is attainable for every odd \\(n\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   Combining the impossibility (3) with the explicit constructions we obtain the\n   complete description\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &\\text{If }n\\text{ is even: }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\},\\\\[2mm]\n   &\\text{If }n\\text{ is odd : }k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n   \\end{aligned}}\n   \\]\n\n   Every listed value of \\(k\\) can be realised by a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines\n   satisfying the covering condition, and no other value of \\(k\\) is possible. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your