nd together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).”\n\n**Verification:**  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) (since \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(b\\ge1\\)). Hence the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\). The lines are distinct and non‑sunny.  \n*Correct.*  \n\n---\n\n### 5. Construction for \\(k=1\\)  \n\n> “Take the \\(n-1\\) diagonal lines \\(x+y=n+1,\\;x+y=n,\\dots ,x+y=3\\) (all non‑sunny) and add the sunny line \\(y=1\\).  \n> The diagonal lines cover all points with \\(a+b\\ge 3\\); the remaining point \\((1,1)\\) lies on \\(y=1\\).”\n\n**Verification:**  \n\n1. **Classification of \\(y=1\\):**  \n   The line \\(y=1\\) is horizontal, i.e. parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis. By the problem’s definition, such a line is **non‑sunny**, not sunny.  \n   Hence the configuration actually contains **zero** sunny lines, contradicting the requirement “exactly \\(k=1\\) sunny line”.  \n\n2. **Coverage claim:**  \n   The diagonal lines indeed cover all points with \\(a+b\\ge3\\). The point \\((1,1)\\) (the only point with \\(a+b=2\\)) is not on any of those diagonals. It does lie on the line \\(y=1\\), but that line is non‑sunny.  \n\nBecause the line labelled “sunny” is in fact non‑sunny, the construction fails to exhibit a configuration with exactly one sunny line.  \n\n*Result:* **Critical Error** – the claimed construction does not satisfy the problem’s condition for \\(k=1\\).  \n\n---\n\n### 6. Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nThe proof is divided into three sub‑steps.\n\n#### 6.1 Reduction  \n\n> “Suppose a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.  \n> Remove all non‑sunny lines; at most \\(n-2\\) of them can be present.  \n> Because a non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\), we may assume (by a simple counting argument) that the non‑sunny lines are the \\(n-2\\) diagonal lines  \n> \\[\n> x+y=n+1,\\;x+y=n,\\dots ,x+y=3 .\n> \\tag{1}\n> \\]  \n> (Any other choice of non‑sunny lines can cover no more points than the set (1