ep 5 to \\(U_k\\); then translate each of the \\(k\\) sunny lines by the vector \\((n-k,0)\\).  The translated lines are still sunny (parallelism is preserved) and together with the vertical lines cover all of \\(T_n\\).  Exactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\n   Hence for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n7. **Collecting the results.**  \n   * \\(k=0\\) – realised by the \\(n\\) vertical lines.  \n   * \\(k=1\\) – realised by \\(n-1\\) vertical lines together with the sunny line \\(y=2x-1\\).  \n   * \\(k=2\\) – impossible (proved in Step 4).  \n   * \\(k\\ge3\\) and \\(k\\le n\\) – realised by the construction of Steps 5–6.  \n\n   Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\; } .\n   \\]\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **non‑sunny** iff it is vertical \\((x=c)\\), horizontal \\((y=c)\\) or has slope \\(-1\\) \\((x+y=c)\\); all other lines are **sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\)\n\nThe sets  \n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid a+b=s\\},\\qquad 2\\le s\\le n+1,\n\\]\n\nare the anti‑diagonals of the triangle; \\(|D_s|=s-1\\le n\\).  \nAny line that is not parallel to an anti‑diagonal meets each \\(D_s\\) in at most one point, hence contains at most one point from each of the \\(n\\) anti‑diagonals, i.e. at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  The same bound holds for vertical, horizontal and slope \\(-1\\) lines, because they intersect each anti‑diagonal in exactly one point (or none).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Realising \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n*\\(k=0\\).*  The \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n\\]\n\nare all non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n*\\(k=1\\).*  Take the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1\n\\]\n\nand the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y=2x-1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(L\\) passes through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,3)\\); the vertical lines cover all points whose first coordinate is at most \\(n-1\\), and the remaining points \\((n,1)\\) and \\((n,2)\\) also lie on \\(L\\).  Thus exactly one sunny line is used.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal F\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly two sunny lines, say \\(S_1,S_2\\).\n\nFor each anti‑diagonal \\(D_s\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\)) let  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha_s=\\#\\{\\,S_i\\mid S_i\\cap D_s\\neq\\varnothing\\,\\}\\in\\{0,1,2\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(\\alpha_s=2\\) for every \\(s\\), then the two sunny lines intersect every anti‑diagonal.  Two distinct non‑parallel lines cannot intersect the same family of parallel anti‑diagonals; consequently the two sunny lines would have to be parallel, which would make them both parallel to some anti‑diagonal – a contradiction because a sunny line is not parallel to any anti‑diagonal.  Hence there exists an index \\(s_0\\) with \\(\\alpha_{s_0}=0\\); i.e. **both** sunny lines miss the anti‑diagonal \\(D_{s_0}\\).\n\nOnly the remaining \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines can meet \\(D_{s_0}\\).  A non‑sunny line meets an anti‑diagonal in at most one point, so at most \\(n-2\\) points of \\(D_{s_0}\\) can be covered.  Since \\(|D_{s_0}|=s_0-1\\ge2\\), at least one point of \\(D_{s_0}\\) stays uncovered, contradicting the hypothesis that \\(\\mathcal F\\) covers all of \\(T_n\\).\n\nTherefore a covering with exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Covering the triangle \\(U_k=T_k\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(k\\ge3\\))\n\nWe construct \\(k\\) distinct